<Base
  title="Methodology | Chip & Cell"
  description="Data sources, update schedule and conventions behind Chip & Cell's Korean semiconductor and battery stock pages."
>
  <h1>Methodology</h1>
  <h2>Data sources</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Prices &amp; indices:</strong> KRX end-of-day market data (via FinanceDataReader).</li>
    <li><strong>Investor flows:</strong> Korea Investment &amp; Securities open API (foreign / institutional / individual net trading). Shown only when available.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Update schedule</h2>
  <p>
    Data pages refresh automatically once per trading day after the Seoul close
    (18:10 KST). The "as of" date on each page is the last completed session.
  </p>
  <h2>Definitions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>52-week position:</strong> where the close sits inside the 252-trading-day low–high range (0% = at the low, 100% = at the high).</li>
    <li><strong>N-day return:</strong> close-to-close percentage change over the last N trading days.</li>
    <li><strong>Median 20-day return:</strong> sector midpoint across our covered names — a rough gauge of sector momentum.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Conventions</h2>
  <p>
    Colors follow the Korean market convention: <span class="up">red = up</span>,
    <span class="down">blue = down</span> — the opposite of Western charts.
  </p>
  <h2>Track record and verification</h2>
  <p>
    Each trading day we record a structured snapshot of every covered stock: closing price,
    position in its 52-week range, distance from its moving averages, and five-session
    cumulative foreign and institutional net buying. Twenty trading sessions later we attach
    what actually happened to the price. These are observations, not forecasts, and results
    are published whether or not they flatter us.
  </p>
  <p>
    Snapshots marked <strong>·retro</strong> were computed retrospectively from historical
    data when the feature launched. They are accurate as facts about those dates, but they
    were not published at the time — only later snapshots carry a timestamped record.
  </p>
  <h2>Value chain and supply relationships</h2>
  <p>
    Each covered company is assigned to one stage of its sector's value chain. Supply
    relationships between covered companies are listed only where we could trace them to a
    citable source, and are separated into those backed by disclosures or company
    announcements and those reported by the trade press. Contract values are as contracted,
    which is not the same as delivered. Relationships that we found to be contradicted by
    the record are deliberately omitted.
  </p>

  <h2>Disclaimer</h2>
  <p>
    All content is for information only and is <strong>not investment advice</strong>.
    Deep dives include model-assisted analysis; despite review, data and text may contain
    errors. Verify against primary sources (KRX, DART filings, company IR) before acting.
  </p>
</Base>
